Z||000000||Zorba||08-20-2000||09:13 PM||||If you have one, can you describe your master clutch cylinder? Does it have the reservoir in front of the pipe or in the middle? I need this info before buying the corresponding m.c. for my car. Thanks in advance.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||08-20-2000||09:46 PM||||An easier way to tell is the first style is aluminum and the hydraulic line comes out behind the plastic res.<P>The second style is cast iron or a steel tube with the line coming out of the top in front of the res.<P>My 85 had the second style, but my 85 parts car had the earlier style so my guess would be yours has the second style.<P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ur2biteme||08-21-2000||03:47 AM||||someone correct me if i'm wrong (ogre?) but i believe the first type (clutch line behind the resivoir) is for the 4 speed isuzu and the second (clutch line in front) is for the isuzu 5 speed.  also, if i remember right, the 86 and 87 (mine) both share the same 5 speed tranny, and my master cylinder is the second type.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Zorba||08-21-2000||11:13 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by ur2biteme:<BR>[B]someone correct me if i'm wrong (ogre?) but i believe the first type (clutch line behind the resivoir) is for the 4 speed isuzu and the second (clutch line in front) is for the isuzu 5 speed.  also, if i  <P>That might explain why my d@#&$m clutch line is kinked somewhat from someone bending it to fit the Behind the reservoir instead of the front. THe bends on my clutch line are not factory...I am not sure if this will affect how much fluid goes out to the slave but I am going to the junkyard to pick up an entire new line just in case. I think someone tried to MAKE it fit into the back of the reservoir. If the fluid isn't flowing smoothly, it might cause some of the problems I was experiencing...Can anyone confirm that the isuzu 5 speed takes the 2nd type?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-21-2000||03:35 PM||||I didn't think it mattered which was used. I have no way to verify if it does or not. You need to know what the bore diameter of each one is. The stroke is the same but if the bores are diferant then it would effect how much fluid moves.....<P>well no that's not entirely correct either..... it would also matter how the reserve fluid enters the cyl..... moving the tank port could effect the usefull stroke of the cylinder.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-21-2000).]||||reg||1
